<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Devices in range share the same second.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The Go has one radio and one antenna, so it divides each second into time slots in the same way as every device in range.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h3 id="second">Each time slot has its channel.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Each time slot uses a channel, meaning a frequency. GPS marks the start of each second to the microsecond. Every skyBlip Go, every skyPost and every FLARM-generation device in range follows the same division of the second.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Time since the start of the second</th><th>Channel</th><th>What is on the channel</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>0 to 200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the end of the time slot that began at 830&nbsp;ms in the previous second. FLARM-generation traffic is still transmitting here, so the device keeps listening. It also sends its callsign here every 10&nbsp;s</td></tr>
        <tr><th>210 to 440&nbsp;ms</th><td>869.525&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the skyPost uplink, meaning messages sent by the ground station: the ADS-B traffic it receives and the weather data it holds, at 200&nbsp;kbit/s. On this channel, the Go listens and never transmits</td></tr>
        <tr><th>450 to 820&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.200&nbsp;MHz</td><td>direct traffic on the lower channel, ADS-L and FLARM-generation together</td></tr>
        <tr><th>830 to 1200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the same on the upper channel, continuing 200&nbsp;ms into the next second</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/second" %>

  <p class="lede">
    Listening on both 868 channels matters. ADS-L alternates channels
    from one transmission to the next. A FLARM-generation device transmits
    on the lower channel in the first half of the second and the upper in the
    second half. A receiver that stayed on one channel would hear half of its
    neighbours and never know which half.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    On those two channels, the Go receives ADS-L and the FLARM-generation format in the same time slot. Both systems put a 32-bit signal pattern, called a sync word, before their frames. The two patterns share their first eighteen signal elements, called chips. The radio detects this shared part. The software then identifies the system from the chips that follow. Each change from one time slot to the next takes
    10&nbsp;ms: 2&nbsp;ms still listening on the old channel for a late burst,
    5&nbsp;ms to retune, and 3&nbsp;ms listening on the new channel before
    its traffic starts. The same 5&nbsp;ms retune covers the change between
    the two bands, which are two modulations and not two frequencies:
    100&nbsp;kbit/s Manchester FSK below, 200&nbsp;kbit/s GMSK above.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The Go transmits for less of the second than it listens. Its positions and Status messages go between 450 and 1000&nbsp;ms after the start of the second. Status messages report what the Go can receive. Positions use 868.2&nbsp;MHz in an even UTC second and
    868.4&nbsp;MHz in an odd one. They are scheduled every second airborne,
    and 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart on the ground. Status goes twice per 20&nbsp;s
    cycle, on the other channel from that second&rsquo;s position. The callsign
    is the exception: every 10&nbsp;s, it goes between 1000 and 1190&nbsp;ms
    on 868.4&nbsp;MHz, in the tail that falls in the next second.
    The Go sends ADS-L frames. This format has no callsign field yet. The Go therefore sends its callsign in a separate message using OGN-Tracker&rsquo;s record format. It receives the FLARM-generation format but never transmits it.
    The ground station transmits on 869.525&nbsp;MHz. This device only listens
    there and never transmits on that frequency. Its planned transmission time
    within each window is calculated from the device address and the current
    second, then recalculated every second to reduce repeated overlaps.
    Before each burst it listens to the channel and, if it is busy, waits
    and listens again within the same window.
    <%= link_to_page "timing", label: "When the Go transmits" %> explains how each unit chooses the second and start time of each transmission.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>GPS aligns four time slots a second to the microsecond</li>
    <li>The device uses 868.200 and 868.400&nbsp;MHz for 375&nbsp;ms each</li>
    <li>It listens for the ground station on 869.525&nbsp;MHz for 235&nbsp;ms</li>
    <li>It decodes ADS-L and the FLARM-generation format within the same time slot</li>
    <li>Each burst lasts about 5&nbsp;ms. A busy channel can delay it</li>
    <li>The Go may transmit for 1&nbsp;% of any hour. It uses about 0.6&nbsp;% airborne or 0.15&nbsp;% on the ground. The firmware enforces this limit</li>
  </ul>
</section>

